  'unit-converter/temperature': {
    intro: `This temperature converter instantly converts between Celsius, Fahrenheit, and Kelvin — the three scales you are most likely to encounter. It is the simplest and most accurate way to get from °C to °F and back, whether you are cooking with a recipe from abroad, checking weather in a different country, or working in science.

The conversion formulas are the heart of it. To convert Celsius to Fahrenheit: °F = (°C × 9/5) + 32. So 20°C is (20 × 1.8) + 32 = 68°F. To convert Fahrenheit to Celsius: °C = (°F − 32) × 5/9. So 68°F is (68 − 32) × 0.556 = 20°C. Two useful anchors: 0°C = 32°F (freezing), and 100°C = 212°F (boiling). And 21°C ≈ 70°F, so room temperature in metric and imperial land are roughly the same.

Kelvin is the SI base unit of temperature and starts at absolute zero, the coldest possible temperature where all molecular motion stops: 0 K = −273.15°C. To convert Celsius to Kelvin, add 273.15; to convert Kelvin to Celsius, subtract 273.15. Kelvin is used in physics, astronomy, and engineering because it has no negative values — useful for doing calculations involving temperature differences and thermochemistry.

